; ------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The same benchmark, in Z80 assembly: a million iterations of a loop.
;
; The BASIC version of this benchmark spends about 3900 T-states per
; iteration, nearly all of it in the interpreter's keyword dispatch --
; every statement is matched by walking a seventeen-entry table, and NEXT
; is the seventeenth one tried.
;
; This is the loop itself and nothing else:
;
;     DEC DE / LD A,D / OR E / JR NZ,inner
;
; which is about 24 T-states per iteration. The gap between the two
; numbers is the price of interpreting, and it is worth having measured
; rather than argued about.
;
; The counters are the same shape as the BASIC benchmark's: 1000 x 1000,
; because a 16-bit counter tops out at 32767. The values are read out of
; the symbol table, so the report cannot claim a million iterations for a
; program that does fewer.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------
OUTER   EQU 1000
INNER   EQU 1000

        ORG 0

START:  LD BC, OUTER          ; the outer counter
OUTERL: LD DE, INNER          ; the inner counter
INNERL: DEC DE                ; one iteration
        LD A, D
        OR E
        JR NZ, INNERL
        DEC BC
        LD A, B
        OR C
        JR NZ, OUTERL
        HALT
